// Military: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== military.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Plywood
genre: military + war
tone: Frost-bitten, comradely and unshowy
era: 1942 | period
setting: A grass airfield behind the southern front | fictional | battlefield
logline: A regiment of women barely out of school flies obsolete canvas-and-plywood crop dusters over enemy lines at night, eight or more sorties each before dawn, cutting their engines to glide in silently, and learns what it costs to be effective.
premise: The aircraft are trainers: open cockpits, no radio, no guns, no parachutes, since the weight is needed for bombs. They carry two. They fly so slowly that the enemy's fighters stall trying to follow. A navigator of nineteen, {zhenya}, who was studying astronomy, is paired with a pilot of twenty-two, {dusya}, who was a tractor mechanic and trusts nothing she has not repaired herself.
synopsis: The first act is formation and contempt. The regiment is made up entirely of women, from commander to armourers; the men's regiments at the next field call them the girls' choir and expect them home in a week. Boots are sizes too large and are stuffed with newspaper. On the first night a crew does not come back, and the commander {major_b} sends the next aircraft up twenty minutes later, because that is the answer. The second act is the method, which they invent. Fly in threes: two draw the searchlights, the third throttles back to idle and glides over the target in silence, so that the only warning is the wind in the wires. Return, rearm in five minutes with the armourers lifting bombs by hand, go again. Eight sorties a night becomes twelve, then eighteen in midwinter darkness. {zhenya} navigates by stars and a stopwatch. {dusya} flies by engine note. They become a single animal, bickering. The enemy gives them a name out of a fairy tale. The men stop joking. The cost is shown without music: an aircraft caught in a cone of light burns like paper in seconds; four crews are lost in one night to a night fighter, and the survivors, back on the field, are counted by their armourer, {katya}, who has learned to recognise each engine. {dusya} is hit and brings the aircraft home on instinct with {zhenya} holding the stick forward from the rear cockpit. In the third act the front moves west and the regiment with it, field to field. {zhenya} is offered a staff post and a return to university. She declines without knowing why. On a night of low cloud they take a new girl's sortie because she is shaking. The film does not show what happens; it stays on the field with {katya}, who is listening for an engine she knows, and at the hour it should come, turns away and begins loading the next aircraft. In the last scene the war is over, and the regiment is paraded and disbanded in the same week. {dusya} is seen years later, driving a tractor, looking up at the sound of a small engine.
question: What does courage look like when it has to be repeated eighteen times a night?
themes: effectiveness as the only argument; work as comradeship; who is remembered
audience: Adults and older teenagers
viewpoint: With {zhenya} in the rear cockpit; the last act shifts to the ground
stakes: Their lives nightly, the regiment's right to exist, and the men they are covering on the ground
conflict: Young women in obsolete aircraft against searchlights, flak, cold, and their own side's condescension
ending: They become one of the most effective night units of the war and many do not return. One of the two leads is lost, off screen. The regiment is disbanded with the peace.
experience: Cold hands, gallows humour, the eerie silence of a glide, and a grief that arrives sideways
look: Blue night, white breath, fragile biplanes against searchlight cones; flowers painted on fuselages; dawn on a muddy field
sound: A small radial engine, then nothing but wind in bracing wires. Bombs hung by hand, clinking. A count of returning engines.
m: prove | institution | self-vs-others | pyrrhic
@ zhenya | Zhenya Markova | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 19 | To get them there and back by the stars | She gave up the sky she loved for this one | The arithmetic of losses | Bookish, precise, shy, stubborn | From student to veteran navigator | Quotes poetry; calls headings | Flying helmet too large, a star chart, a stopwatch on a string
@ dusya | Dusya Pavlenko | co-lead | ally | human | she/her | 22 | Every sortie, and the engine sound right | A village behind enemy lines is hers | She will not let anyone else take a turn | Blunt, funny, superstitious about tools | From sceptic of a "student" to her other half | Peasant bluntness, swears at the engine | Oil under the nails, boots stuffed with newspaper
@ major_b | Major Sokolova | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 30 | A regiment that cannot be disbanded for failure | Women asked for this and she must make it work | Each loss is someone's daughter and an argument for the doubters | Calm, exacting, never raises her voice | From proving a point to burying her girls | Quiet orders | Greatcoat, a map case, a thermos
@ katya | Katya | supporting | ally | human | she/her | 20 | Five minutes to rearm, every time | She hangs each bomb by hand | She knows each engine by ear | Small, strong, wordless about her feelings | From armourer to the one who counts them home | Counts under her breath | Padded jacket, frost-split hands
~ zhenya - dusya | navigator and pilot | a student and a mechanic, mutually unimpressed | one animal with two cockpits
~ zhenya - major_b | officer cadet and commander | an order to rest | a staff post declined
~ dusya - katya | pilot and armourer | bombs and curses | the engine she listens for
# The Choir | EXT | Grass Airfield | DAY | zhenya, dusya, major_b, katya | Arrive | Trainers with open cockpits. Boots stuffed with newspaper. Men from the next field laughing at the fence. {dusya} looks at her navigator and asks if she can at least read a compass | | 5 | crowd=120, vehicles=plywood biplanes
# The Choir | EXT | Grass Airfield | NIGHT | major_b, zhenya, dusya | Go anyway | First night. One crew does not return. Twenty minutes later the commander sends the next aircraft. {zhenya}'s hand shakes on the stopwatch | The first loss | 6 | crowd=60, vehicles=plywood biplanes
# The Glide | INT/EXT | Rear Cockpit, Over The Line | NIGHT | zhenya, dusya | Cut the engine | Two aircraft take the searchlights. Theirs throttles to idle. Silence, wind in the wires, a river by starlight. She counts. She pulls the release | The method works | 8 | vehicles=plywood biplane, stunts=aerial sequence, vfx=searchlights and flak
# The Glide | EXT | Grass Airfield | NIGHT | katya, dusya, zhenya | Rearm | Five minutes. Bombs lifted by hand under the wings. Tea passed up into the cockpit. Again. Again. The eighth time, dawn is grey behind them | | 5 | crowd=40, vehicles=plywood biplanes, props=bombs
# The Cost | INT/EXT | Rear Cockpit, In The Cone | NIGHT | zhenya, dusya | Watch it burn | The aircraft ahead is caught in white light and is gone like paper. Then theirs is hit. {dusya} slumps. {zhenya} leans forward on the dual stick and flies by her voice | {dusya} is wounded | 8 | vehicles=plywood biplanes, stunts=aerial sequence, vfx=aircraft burning
# The Cost | EXT | Grass Airfield | DAWN | katya, major_b | Count | Four crews in one night. {katya} names each engine she does not hear. The commander writes the letters herself | | 5 | crowd=60
# The Last Sortie | EXT | Forward Airfield | NIGHT | zhenya, dusya, katya | Take her turn | Low cloud. A new girl shaking. {dusya} says they will take it. {zhenya} has a staff post in her pocket, undelivered. She climbs in | They take the extra sortie | 5 | vehicles=plywood biplanes
# The Last Sortie | EXT | Forward Airfield | NIGHT | katya, major_b | Listen | The field. Engines returning, each one known. An hour. One she is waiting for. She turns and lifts a bomb onto the next rack | One of them does not return | 6 | crowd=40, vehicles=plywood biplanes
# The Last Sortie | EXT | Wheat Field, Years Later | DAY | dusya | Look up | A tractor. A small engine somewhere overhead. A woman in a headscarf stops and looks up until it has gone | | 3 | vehicles=tractor
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from a women's night bomber regiment of 1942 to 1945, with invented people
